Without clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC_COARSE), rpc_current_time() falls
back to time(NULL) * 1000, which steps in whole seconds. That is the case
on Windows and Apple platforms:

- The first privileged source port is that time modulo 400, so every
  process started at port 512 or 712. A connection from an earlier run
  still in TIME_WAIT on one of those ports made the connect, and so the
  mount, fail.
- Every timeout is lengthened by a second to make up for the steps, so a
  1 s timeout took 1 to 2 s.

Use GetTickCount64() on Windows and clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC) on
Apple platforms, and drop the extra second there. The context is kept to
two lines, so that the patch also applies after 03-wasm-monotonic-clock.

--- a/lib/init.c
+++ b/lib/init.c
@@ -62,5 +62,12 @@
 uint64_t rpc_current_time(void)
 {
-#ifdef HAVE_CLOCK_GETTIME
+#if defined(WIN32)
+	return GetTickCount64();
+#elif defined(__APPLE__)
+	struct timespec ts;
+
+	clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &ts);
+	return (uint64_t)ts.tv_sec * 1000 + ts.tv_nsec / 1000000;
+#elif defined(HAVE_CLOCK_GETTIME)
 	struct timespec tp;
 
--- a/lib/libnfs-sync.c
+++ b/lib/libnfs-sync.c
@@ -205,5 +205,5 @@
 	if (rpc->timeout > 0) {
 		timeout = rpc_current_time() + rpc->timeout;
-#ifndef HAVE_CLOCK_GETTIME
+#if !defined(HAVE_CLOCK_GETTIME) && !defined(WIN32) && !defined(__APPLE__)
 		/* If we do not have GETTIME we fallback to time() which
 		 * has 1s granularity for its timestamps.
